    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mangrai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mangrai.jpg" alt="Mangrai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mangrai_Statue,_Pho_Khun_Intersection,_Chiang_Rai_(2026)_-_img_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chainwit., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lan Na, "a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields", grew out of the Tai principality of Ngoenyang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a> area of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a>, became ruler of Ngoenyang around 1261 and set about uniting the small Tai <em>mueang</em> of the region. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> as his new seat in 1262, according to the chronicles, and extended his control south and west. His decisive step was the conquest of Hariphunchai, the old Mon kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a>, whose Buddhist culture and fertile Ping valley gave the young state both wealth and prestige; the chronicles disagree on the date, but 1292 is the most commonly given. In 1296 Mangrai founded a new capital nearby at Chiang Mai, "new city", on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ping-river">Ping River</a>. Tradition holds that he chose the site in consultation with his allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng of Sukhothai</a>, with whom he had sworn friendship. Mangrai also faced the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, which had overrun the Dali kingdom and attacked Pagan; Yuan expeditions sent against Lan Na in the first years of the fourteenth century achieved little, and the kingdom later sent tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. By Mangrai's death, around 1311, Lan Na controlled the main valleys of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok.jpg" alt="Wat Suan Dok" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok">Wat Suan Dok</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Suan_Dok_2026_(325013).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Setawut, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lan Na's economy rested on wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming in its mountain valleys, made possible by the <em>muang fai</em> system of weirs and channels that diverted streams to the fields. Villages that shared a weir cooperated to maintain it under customary rules, and the irrigation system was regulated by law. The kingdom's valleys were separated by forested hills inhabited by other peoples, including Lawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-people">Karen</a>, who traded forest products with the lowlanders. Chiang Mai and other towns lay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> that linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the Thai lowlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, along which traders carried goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, metalware, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and forest products. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> dominated cultural life. In 1369 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kue-na">Kue Na</a> invited the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumana-thera">Sumana</a> from Sukhothai, who established a Sri Lankan-derived ordination lineage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok">Wat Suan Dok</a>, and in the fifteenth century a rival reform movement brought new ordinations directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Lan Na monks wrote in Pali and developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham</a>, or Dhamma, script, used for religious texts and later for the local language across the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> region. The fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries produced chronicles such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinakalamali">Jinakalamali</a></em> of Ratanapanna and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camadevivamsa">Camadevivamsa</a></em> of Bodhiramsi, which made Chiang Mai one of the main centres of Pali learning in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tilokarat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tilokarat.jpg" alt="Tilokarat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tilokarat">Tilokarat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilokaraj.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Security Thainam, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lan Na's armies were raised from the free men of its valleys under the command of the king and the rulers of its <em>mueang</em>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> were the core of royal forces. Chiang Mai was protected by a square brick wall with a moat, much of which survives, and other towns had similar defences. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> built the kingdom through conquest, taking Hariphunchai and extending his authority to neighbouring principalities, and his successors absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> in 1338. The kingdom's greatest military figure was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilokarat">Tilokarat</a> (r. 1441–1487), who seized the throne from his father and then brought Nan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a> under direct control around 1449. He fought a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a>, who moved his own residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> in the north to direct the struggle; the fighting over the borderlands of the former Sukhothai kingdom lasted, with interruptions, from the 1450s to the 1470s and ended without decisive change. Lan Na also clashed with the Shan states to the west and with Lan Xang to the east. In the sixteenth century its military strength faded as rival factions fought over the throne, and in 1558 the Burmese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> captured Chiang Mai almost without resistance, beginning two centuries of Burmese domination.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha.jpg" alt="Emerald Buddha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_(51).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">กสิณธร ราชโอรส, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a>'s dynasty ruled Chiang Mai directly and governed the other principalities through relatives and local lords, who held their <em>mueang</em> in return for loyalty, tribute and troops. As in other Tai states, power was personal and tended to fragment when kings were weak, and succession disputes were frequent. Mangrai is credited with a law code, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangraisat">Mangraisat</a></em>, which survives in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> versions and deals with matters such as land, irrigation, debt, marriage, theft and the fines owed for injuries; how much of it dates to his reign is uncertain. Kingship was closely bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Kings endowed monasteries, sponsored the copying of scriptures and enshrined relics, and the most venerated images of the region passed through Chiang Mai: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> was kept there from 1468, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilokarat">Tilokarat</a>'s reign, until the early 1550s. In 1477 Tilokarat convened a council of monks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chet-yot">Wat Chet Yot</a> to revise the Pali canon, counted in Thai tradition as the Eighth Buddhist Council. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-mueang-kaeo">Phra Mueang Kaeo</a> (r. 1495–1525) royal patronage of scholarship reached its height. During the Burmese period from 1558, Lan Na was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers or Burmese governors, and the local nobility of each <em>mueang</em> retained considerable authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-singh.jpg" alt="Wat Phra Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-singh">Wat Phra Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Sing,_Chiang_Mai_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ..., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lan Na created a distinct northern Thai civilisation that remains visible in the language, script, architecture and customs of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and neighbouring regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a> spread from Chiang Mai to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Sipsong Panna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-people">Tai Khun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> and Lan Xang, where it became the standard script for Buddhist texts. Lan Na Pali literature, especially the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinakalamali">Jinakalamali</a></em>, is a main source for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in the region. Its monastic lineages and the council of 1477 influenced Buddhist practice across the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. The architecture of Chiang Mai, with its tiered roofs, stucco decoration and temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-singh">Wat Phra Singh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chedi-luang">Wat Chedi Luang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chet-yot">Wat Chet Yot</a>, defines the Lan Na style still used in the north today. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangraisat">Mangraisat</a></em> was copied and adapted for centuries and provides rare evidence of customary law in a Tai state. Lan Na's long struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and its subjection to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> also shaped the political map of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: its absorption by Siam in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries largely set the present border between Thailand and Myanmar. Northern Thai identity, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-thai-language">Kham Mueang</a> language, still draws on Lan Na's history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/setthathirath.jpg" alt="Setthathirath" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setthathirat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alcyon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-mueang-kaeo">Phra Mueang Kaeo</a>'s death in 1525 Lan Na entered a period of court factionalism. In 1545 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ket-klao">Ket Klao</a> was killed, and the nobles invited the young prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath of Lan Xang</a>, whose mother was a Chiang Mai princess, to take the throne; he left in 1547 to claim the Lao throne, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> was taken to Lan Xang. Weakened and divided, Lan Na fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> in 1558. Burmese kings first ruled through Lan Na <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisutthithewi">Queen Wisutthithewi</a>, and from 1579 through Bayinnaung's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawrahta-minsaw">Nawrahta Minsaw</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> disintegrated, Lan Na briefly drifted towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, but in 1614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo</a> kingdom reasserted control. A revolt in 1727 restored independence to Chiang Mai for several decades, though the old kingdom was by then divided among rival centres. In 1763 the new Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> reconquered Chiang Mai and used it as a base for its war on Ayutthaya, which fell in 1767. Harsh Burmese rule provoked northern lords, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawila">Kawila of Lampang</a> and Phraya Chaban, to side with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> of Thonburi, whose forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Chao Phraya Chakri</a> helped take Chiang Mai in 1775. Lan Na's principalities thereafter became vassals of Siam, later forming the Kingdom of Chiang Mai, which was fully incorporated into Siam by 1899.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lan Na was the principal kingdom of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> for nearly five centuries, a centre of Pali scholarship whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a>, law code and architecture defined a northern Thai civilisation that survived Burmese domination and still shapes the identity of the region today.</em></p>

